# Learning Goal – Realise (Sprint 2)

## Learning Question

**How can I implement and test a functional smart pedestrian crossing subsystem with two opposite crossings using an ESP32-S3, based on my design, ensuring correct behavior and reliability?**

---

# S — Situation

In Sprint 2 of the City Sim project, I will move from design to implementation of my smart pedestrian crossing subsystem.

Based on the previously created design, I will build a system with two opposite pedestrian crossings, each with a button and signal lights, controlled by an ESP32-S3.

Since I am still relatively new to embedded systems, this phase will require me to apply my design in practice and learn how to translate a conceptual design into a working prototype.


# T — Task

I aim to:
- Build the pedestrian crossing system on a breadboard prototype.
- Correctly connect all components (buttons, LEDs, resistors) to the ESP32-S3.
- Implement the control logic in code based on my design.
- Test the system to ensure it behaves correctly and consistently.
- Identify and fix any issues in wiring, logic, or timing.

# A — Action

To achieve this, I will:

1. Build the Circuit
- Assemble the components on a breadboard based on my design.
- Connect LEDs, buttons, and resistors to the correct ESP32 pins.
2. Implement Control Logic in Code
- Translate the designed system logic into working code.
- Program the ESP32-S3 to control LEDs and read button inputs.
3. Test System Behavior
- Test how the system reacts to button presses.
- Verify that both pedestrian crossings are synchronized correctly.
- Check timing of red/green signals.
4. Debug and Improve
- Identify issues such as incorrect wiring, logic errors, or timing problems.
- Adjust code or connections to improve system behavior.
5. Validate Functionality
- Ensure the system works reliably under repeated use.
- Confirm that the system meets the intended design and behavior.